<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taranis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taranus, Tanarus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taranis, sometimes Taranus or Tanarus, was a Celtic thunder god known from literary sources and from inscriptions dating between the 4th century BCE and the 3rd century CE; his name comes from Proto-Celtic *torano-, thunder. The Roman poet Lucan names him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esus">Esus</a> and Teutates as gods to whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> sacrificed humans, and early medieval scholia on Lucan add that his victims were burned in a hollow wooden container, a rite compared with the wicker man described by Caesar. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> equated him with Jupiter, as inscriptions confirm. Some scholars identify him with the Celtic wheel god, though no direct evidence links the two.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taranis.jpg" alt="Taranis" width="330" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taranis_Jupiter_with_wheel_and_thunderbolt_Le_Chatelet_Gourzon_Haute_Marne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Taranis derives from Proto-Celtic *torano-, thunder, which comes from the Proto-Indo-European root *(s)tenh₂-, to thunder. It is cognate with words for thunder in Old Irish (torann), Old Breton (taran) and Middle Welsh (taran), and corresponds etymologically to the name of the Germanic god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Donar</a>, that is Thor. Peter Jackson has conjectured that both names arose from an epithet of the Proto-Indo-European thunder god *Perkʷūnos. In Celtic the word seems to have changed from *tonaro- to *torano-, and a dedication to Iovi Tanaro, Jupiter Tanaris, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> confirms that the older form existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Roman poet Lucan, in the Pharsalia, begun about 61 CE, names Teutates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esus">Esus</a> and Taranis as gods to whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a>, and says the altar of Taranis was no kinder than that of Diana of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. Lucan never travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and no earlier source names these gods. Some scholars, such as Jan de Vries, have argued that the three formed a triad, but there is little other evidence tying them together.</p><p>Medieval scholia on Lucan, thought to contain older material, say that people were burned in a wooden container as sacrifices to Taranis, a rite Miranda Green linked to the wicker man. One commentary, the Commenta, calls him leader of wars and chief of the heavenly gods, and says he was appeased with human heads, and it describes a later shift from human to animal sacrifice. All three commentaries equate him with Jupiter, as inscriptions do; only the Commenta also gives Dis Pater, the Roman god of the underworld, an equation that is obscure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Because Taranis was equated with Jupiter, some scholars, among them Pierre Lambrechts, Jean-Jacques Hatt and Anne Ross, have identified him with the Celtic wheel god, known only from images showing a spoked wheel together with Jupiter's thunderbolt. No inscription links Taranis with wheel imagery, and Green argues that the wheel god was a solar deity distinct from the thunder god.</p><p>The inscriptions that name him date between the 4th century BCE and the 3rd century CE. The spelling Taranus is much commoner than Taranis, and a form meaning son or descendant of Taranus appears on two inscriptions from Germania Superior. Scholars draw opposite conclusions from them: Marion Euskirchen calls the evidence scanty and suggests limited importance, while Hofeneder says he is attested surprisingly often and was worshipped in large parts of Keltiké over a long period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taranis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Taranis</b> was a sky god of thunder, the wheel and the bull; Lugus a god of skill and craft; Sirona and Borvo healers tied to sacred springs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar matched the gods of Gaul with Roman ones, above all Mercury, whom he calls the inventor of all arts, and the poet Lucan names three Gaulish gods, Teutates, Esus and <b>Taranis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped many gods, among them the thunder god <b>Taranis</b>, Lugus, a god of skills and crafts, and the horse goddess Epona, held ceremonies in sacred groves and offered treasured things in rivers, wetlands and shafts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
